{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Boolean Logic and Boolean Algebra", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 6, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A1.1", "type": "level"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you hear when talking about Boolean logic.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"content": "Exchange information about logic, its principles, and methods in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Understands key information in simple written and spoken texts about topics related to software programming and its history. Writes basic ideas on programming concepts and/or programming history.", "type": "secondary_1"}]}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob06re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Simple Connectors", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use <b>connectors</b> to join or link words or sentences. They are of great importance to create coherence and cohesion when talking or writing. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "The most common <b>connectors</b> that we use in English are <b>AND</b>, <b>OR</b> and <b>BUT</b>. Let's check how they work: </br></br>We use the connector <b>AND</b> to join two sentences that are grammatically similar or when we list different elements.</br></br>On the other hand, we use the connector <b>OR</b> to express an alternative or an option. </br></br>Finally, we use the connector <b>BUT</b> to contrast ideas. </br></br>For example:</br><i>Juan <b>and</b> Carlos are experts on systems theory.</br>  We can use the name \u201csystems theory\u201d <b>or</b> \u201csystems science\u201d. You decide which one you want to use.  </br>Carla wants to work with algebra, <b>but</b> she is bad at it. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When we use the two valued logic, we create expressions, which are a combination of logical operands and operators.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "In simple words, operands are basically statements (which are true or false).", "type": "example"}, {"content": "As the Boolean logic does not take two values but only one, we also name it the two valued logic.", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Present Simple Negative", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use the <b>present simple</b> to talk about habits or facts.  ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Many people don't get to understand a lot about two different but important programming concepts: Boolean logic and Boolean algebra.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Many people don't get the idea of the two valued logic or the Boolean logic without examples, so let's check some.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We can use the <b>present simple</b> in both, its affirmative or negative form. When we use it in its <b>negative form</b>, we refer to elements that are not facts or don't make part of our routines. The structure that we use to write negative sentences using this tense is: </br></br><center>Subject + auxiliary verb \u201cdo\u201d or \u201cdoes\u201d* + not + verb in base form + complement. </center></br>Don't forget that we use the auxiliary verb \u201c<b>do</b>\u201d for the pronouns I, you, we, and they. On the other hand, we use the auxiliary verb \u201c<b>does</b>\u201d for the pronouns he, she, and it.</br></br>Examples:</br><i> I <b>don't understand</b> anything about algebra.</br>  She <b>does not study</b> at home, that is why she is so bad with numbers.</i> </br></br>We can also use imperatives, orders or commands, to express ideas in the negative form of the present simple tense. For this, we omit the pronoun and start the sentence with the verb. </br></br>For example:<i></br><b>Don't* forget</b> to study Boolean logic for the exam.  </br><b>Do not come </b>until you understand what valued logic is. </i></br></br>*It is important to know that when we use imperatives, it is not common to use the auxiliary verb does.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "operand", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the element we use to make a mathematical operation, for example numbers or statements", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In simple words, operands are basically statements (which are true or false).", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob06aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "operator", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a convention people use to express a mathematical operation", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In arithmetic, we say that operands are numbers and operators are mathematical actions like addition, subtraction, multiplication and division.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob06aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "value", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a quantity represented by a symbol", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: As the Boolean logic does not take two values but only one, we also name it the two valued logic.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob06aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "expression", "type": "name"}, {"content": "numbers, symbols, operands and operators grouped together that show the value of something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: When we use the two valued logic, we create expressions, which are a combination of logical operands and operators.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n1le01ob06aud04", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "tar